# What survives when a paper leaves the lab: a pre-registered re-test of {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a €{{rig_eur}} rig

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

Robot-learning papers are mostly shown in simulation, on costly arms or on data-centre GPUs. We re-tested the
central claim of {{papers}} papers on a low-cost arm and an edge GPU, running the cheapest baseline first and fixing a
pass mark before the run. Of the {{c_paper}} claims taken from the papers, {{c_paper_repro}} reproduce and {{c_paper_fail}} fail; {{c_ours}} further rows compare against our own baselines. The failures we see
fall into three proposed classes: methods that miss a real-time budget on the edge GPU, zero-shot judges beaten by a
trivial signal, and latent planners beaten by the commanded joint positions. We then audited our own
pre-registration by commit order, recomputed the early gates with one statistic, and rebuilt every published number
from its stored rows. Whether the bar preceded the result is provable for most gates, not for all, and the class
assignment awaits a blind second reader; both limits are stated with their counts. Total compute: ${{cost}}.

## 1. The register

A claim shown on a Franka-class arm, in simulation or on a data-centre GPU does not say whether it holds on the
hardware most students and small labs own. The register is one entry per paper: the claim as the paper states it,
the pass mark we fixed for our rig, our result and a verdict, with the numbers recomputable from published rows. It
rests on three conditions. **Accessible hardware:** an SO-101 arm and a Jetson Orin NX 16 GB, €{{rig_eur}} for the
parts with a stated price ({{rig_unpriced}} smaller parts not yet priced). **Cheapest baseline first:** before a
learned model is credited, the trivial signal it must beat (copy-last, the commanded joints, episode length) is run on
the same rows. **Bar before the run:** the pass mark is committed before the result; §2 audits how far that holds.
Of the {{papers}} papers, {{rig_sim}} were shown in simulation, {{rig_gt20k}} on rigs above $20k, {{rig_5to20k}} at
$5–20k, {{rig_lt5k}} below $5k and {{rig_unstated}} on an unstated setup.

## 2. Method, and an audit of our own pre-registration

Each gate of the experiment log has a bar and a result; we find, in git history, the first commit containing each and
classify the gate by commit order alone (`git merge-base --is-ancestor`), never from a note or a timestamp. Of the
{{pr_rows}} gate rows of the log as of 2026-10-31,
**{{pr_bar_first}} are bar-first, {{pr_same_commit}} same-commit, {{pr_result_first}} result-first, {{pr_no_bar}} no-bar and {{pr_not_run}} not-run**
(a bar, never a result). The {{pr_today}}
rows of the log today give {{pt_bar_first}} / {{pt_same_commit}} / {{pt_result_first}} / {{pt_no_bar}} / {{pt_not_run}}.
"Same-commit" means the bar and the result first appear in the same commit of this repository; it is not evidence
that the result came first, nor that it did not.

A first pass by text search alone had read {{v1_bar_first}} bar-first, {{v1_same_commit}} same-commit,
{{v1_no_bar}} no-bar and {{v1_unresolved}} unresolved. Tracing each same-commit gate to its own protocol, plan,
scorer or specification moved {{sc_to_bf}} to bar-first and {{sc_to_nb}} to no-bar (the bar cell itself declares
none), and left {{sc_stay}}. Those upgrades rest on a bar text found in an earlier commit of a file tied to the gate;
the evidence line for each is in the audit table, so a reader can check it. The earlier bar-first rows were not
re-audited for a result elsewhere in the history. One gate added on 2026-11-01 (the reward-model progress test) is
same-commit by text search, but its protocol (`{{top_protocol}}`) predates the log commit; its result commit was not
audited, so it stays same-commit and is an open candidate.

**One statistic.** The early gates mixed point estimates and other intervals. We recomputed {{rc_rows}} of them on
the episode bootstrap ({{rc_resamples}} resamples, percentile 95 % CI) from stored rows. {{rc_flips}} verdicts flip
somewhere inside their interval: the oscillation rate among failed episodes ({{osc_est}} %, CI {{osc_lo}}–{{osc_hi}} %,
bar ≥ {{osc_bar}} %, n = {{osc_n}}; {{osc_verdict}} on the point), a judge's accuracy ({{acc_est}} %, CI
{{acc_lo}}–{{acc_hi}} %, bar ≥ {{acc_bar}} %, n = {{acc_n}}; {{acc_verdict}}) and a judge threshold's false-intervention
rate ({{tau_est}} %, CI {{tau_lo}}–{{tau_hi}} %, bar ≤ {{tau_bar}} %, n = {{tau_n}}; {{tau_verdict}}, but the threshold
was fitted to meet that rate, so its interval is descriptive). The register keeps the point verdicts and reports the
three as fragile.

**Reproducibility of this study.** Every published number was recomputed from its published rows: {{l0}} / {{l0}}
write-ups pass. From a fresh clone with no credentials, {{l1_repro}} gates rerun cold and reproduce ({{l1_exact}}
exactly); {{l1_env}} are blocked by one unpublished software environment and {{l1_skip}} need a larger GPU, a
private checkpoint, the arm or an unstated step. The register's citations and paper-side fields were audited against
the papers' PDFs and the fixes are merged; of the {{ps_ids}} arXiv ids the published register names, the missing ones in our papers mirror
belong to scheduled tests, and one tested paper, which has no arXiv id, was not checked against its PDF.

