{{c_paper}} paper claims alone, {{c_paper_repro}} reproduce and {{c_paper_fail}} fail.

## 4. Three failure classes

We propose three classes for the {{fail_rows}} failed claim rows. **A, edge latency:** the method may work, but on
the edge GPU it misses the real-time budget the claim needs; an adaptive chunking method takes {{ex_a_ours}} per
decision against a budget of {{ex_a_bar}}. **B, zero-shot judges:** a model used without training on this rig to
judge success, failure or progress is no better than a trivial signal; a learned reward model detects failures at
AUROC {{ex_b_ours}} where episode length alone reaches {{ex_b_len}}. **C, latent planners beaten by joints:** a
learned latent used to plan, rank or track progress does worse than the commanded joints or a copy-last /
action-only baseline; a latent world model ranks the executed chunk first in {{ex_c_ours}} of anchors, against a bar
of {{ex_c_bar}} and {{ex_c_base}} for an action-only baseline. The three examples are illustrative. The assignment of
every failed row is pending: the owner labels the {{fail_rows}} rows first and commits the labels, then a blind second
reader labels the same form, and the classes are reported as reproducible only if Cohen's κ ≥ {{kappa_bar}} (the bar
was fixed before any reading).

## 5. Limitations

One rig, one arm, one operator: a failure here is a rig finding, not a refutation of the paper's own setting. The
verdicts were labelled by one person, at rest, not always blind to the arm. {{pr_same_commit}} gates are same-commit
and {{pr_no_bar}} have no bar, so for {{pr_unprov}} of {{pr_rows}} gates the order of bar and result is not provable
from this repository; the commit-order audit is mechanical, but its {{sc_to_bf}} upgrades rest on matching a bar text, and the
second-reader check of the classes is the only independent check in the study, not yet done. {{rc_flips}} verdicts are
fragile under the bootstrap. Only {{l1_repro}} gates were rerun cold. The closest statement of our thesis
[@2610.40026] scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, among them reproduction evidence and deployment affordability; its reproduction score counts audit milestones (configuration mapping, task records, coverage, training records, seeds), not a rerun of each claim. We give one pass/fail per claim on a €1,350 rig, with the bar committed before the run.

## 6. Related work

A standardized real-world benchmark on the same arm reports a failure and recovery taxonomy for VLA policies
[@2606.08881]; a fleet of SO-101 cells evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. A unified harness decouples VLA
inference from simulation benchmarks [@2603.13966], and a benchmark of demonstration quality studies one method
family empirically [@2610.18869]. These evaluate policies; we re-test the claims behind methods (judges, world
models, latency) against bars fixed before the run, and audit our own pre-registration.

